# Project Rules — SaaS Project Management Platform

These rules define how this project must be built and maintained. They apply to backend, frontend, database, testing, UI, security, performance, and developer workflow.

**Project stack:** Laravel 12, PHP 8.3, MySQL, Redis, Vue 3, Pinia, Vue Router, Tailwind CSS, shadcn-vue.

---

# 1. Core Project Principles

## 1.1 Build for production quality

- Avoid quick hacks; prefer clean architecture over shortcuts.
- Keep code readable, maintainable, and scalable.
- Every major feature should be modular and extensible.
- Do not write throwaway code.

## 1.2 Multi-tenant safety is mandatory

- All workspace-owned data must always be scoped correctly.
- Never return data from another workspace.
- Every query involving projects, boards, tasks, members, notifications, or logs must respect workspace boundaries.
- Backend authorization must enforce tenant isolation, not just frontend checks.

## 1.3 Thin controllers, strong domain logic

- Controllers must remain thin.
- Validation → Form Requests. Business logic → services/actions. Authorization → policies.
- Repeated logic must be extracted into reusable classes.
- Models should not become bloated with unrelated logic.

## 1.4 Consistency over cleverness

- Follow existing naming conventions and the same structure across modules.
- Do not introduce new architectural patterns unless clearly justified.
- Prefer boring, predictable, maintainable code.

---

# 2. Backend Rules

## 2.1 Structure

Organize by module: Auth, Workspace, Members, Projects, Boards, Tasks, Comments, Attachments, Notifications, Activity Logs. Each module: Model, Controller, Form Request, Service/Action, Policy, Resource/DTO, Routes, Tests.

## 2.2 Laravel conventions

- Use Eloquent relationships, Form Requests, API Resources, Policies, Jobs, Events/Listeners.
- Use database transactions for multi-step write operations.

## 2.3 Validation

- Never trust frontend validation alone; every write endpoint must validate input.
- Use enums or constants for status, role, priority. Validate file types, sizes, upload limits.

## 2.4 Authorization

- Every protected action must be authorized on the backend.
- Use policies for create, view, update, delete, archive, invite, assign, reorder, comment.
- Authorization must respect workspace membership and user role.

## 2.5 Service layer

- Complex business logic in service/action classes; one clear job per service.
- Services must be reusable, testable, and receive explicit arguments (not raw request unless justified).

## 2.6 Database write safety

- Use transactions for multi-table writes; fail safely; no partial data on workflow failure.
- Reordering (boards/tasks) must be atomic. Use foreign keys; soft deletes only when they add product value.

## 2.7 API design

- Resource-oriented, consistent JSON, proper HTTP methods, pagination for lists.
- Status codes: 200 success, 201 created, 204 no-content delete, 422 validation, 403 forbidden, 404 not found.

---

# 3. Database Rules

- Table names plural; explicit indexed foreign keys; consistent status-like fields.
- Index: foreign keys, workspace_id, due_date, status, priority, ordering fields where queried.
- Migrations: one clear change each; never modify old migrations; use follow-up migrations; define cascade intentionally.

---

# 4. Frontend Rules

- Vue 3 Composition API; small focused components; composables for repeated logic; Pinia only for shared state.
- Use shadcn-vue and Tailwind consistently; one responsibility per component; typed props/emits.
- Forms: loading, success, error states; disable submit during submission; confirmation for destructive actions.
- Fast and responsive; skeletons/loaders; meaningful empty states; toast/inline feedback.

---

# 5. State Management (Pinia)

- Use Pinia only for shared/cross-page state. Stores: auth, workspace context, notifications, shared filters, UI preferences. Keep stores focused by domain.

---

# 6. Testing Rules

- Major business logic must be tested; new features are not complete without tests; bug fixes should include regression tests.
- Backend: Feature tests for endpoints; Unit tests for isolated logic; test success and failure paths; test cross-workspace denial and role restrictions.
- Frontend (if added): page rendering, form validation, loading/error states, board/task interactions; test behavior, not implementation.
- Use factories; realistic scenarios; independent, reliable tests.

---

# 7. Code Quality

- Readable first; avoid deep nesting; explicit names; no dead or commented-out code; no debug dumps in commits.
- Good names: CreateWorkspaceAction, AssignTaskMembersAction, ProjectMemberPolicy. Bad: HelperService, DoTaskThing.
- Avoid massive classes/components; split when responsibilities grow.

---

# 8. Security

- Validate all input; authorize all protected actions; sanitize/validate uploads; prevent mass-assignment; never trust client-provided workspace/role blindly.
- File uploads: validate mime/size; safe storage; safe stored filenames.
- Destructive actions: require confirmation; log in activity; restrict high-risk to workspace owners/admins.

---

# 9. Performance

- Avoid N+1; eager load; paginate large lists; use indexes intentionally.
- Frontend: lazy load where helpful; avoid unnecessary rerenders; keep board interactions smooth.
- Redis queues for: email notifications, heavy activity processing, expensive async work; jobs retry-safe and idempotent where practical.

---

# 10. Activity Log & Notifications

- Log important user actions with human-readable messages; include actor, workspace, entity, action type.
- Notifications: relevant (assignments, mentions, important updates); centralized creation; read/unread; structured for frontend.

---

# 11. Git & Docs

- Small focused commits; conventional style (feat:, fix:, refactor:, test:, ui:). Feature branches; merge when stable.
- Document architecture, README, setup, env vars, major modules; comments explain why, not what.

---

# 12. Definition of Done

Feature complete when: requirements implemented, backend validation and authorization exist, tenant scoping correct, UI states and error handling complete, tests added, no debug code, conventions followed.

---

# 13. Avoid

- Bypassing authorization; business logic in controllers; giant random “helper” classes; duplicated code; hardcoded workspace assumptions; skipping tests for critical flows; inconsistent UI; TODO-based unfinished critical logic; premature optimization; happy-path-only features.

---

# 14. Preferred Build Order

1. Documentation → 2. Database schema → 3. Backend foundation → 4. Frontend foundation → 5. Auth → 6. Workspace & members → 7. Projects → 8. Boards & columns → 9. Tasks → 10. Comments & attachments → 11. Notifications → 12. Activity logs → 13. Dashboard → 14. Polish → 15. Tests & refactoring.

---

# 15. Standard

This project must feel like a real product, clean codebase, scalable system, strong portfolio piece — not a tutorial clone, rushed CRUD demo, or unfinished prototype.
